---
/**
 * Reframe 2026-08-04. The 2026-08 standard-tier rewrite was factually clean
 * but framed this as feature work on an existing app. It was 0->1: there was
 * no Impress patient app, the owner proposed it and argued its economics.
 * Owner-approved this pass: FLAGSHIP tier (superseding the 2026-08-03
 * standard approval), Role prop "Founding Product Designer -> Design Lead",
 * and the new claims recorded in intake sheet section 7 (2026-08-04
 * addendum) — origin, v1 scope, both rejected alternatives, and the
 * design-function influence artifact.
 *
 * Internal figures stay directional (adoption, savings, per-visit cost,
 * store ratings) per the owner's publication rule; the Series B is the one
 * exact public figure and carries its source link.
 *
 * ASSETS, verified by opening the files this pass:
 * - `ideation.png` is NOT brainstorm output (its old alt was wrong) — it is a
 *   team selfie, seven identifiable masked faces and a name badge. Never put
 *   it on this page. It stays in public/ unreferenced by owner decision
 *   (IM-39, 2026-08-04, made in full knowledge of what it shows) — do not
 *   "fix" that in a later pass without asking again.
 * - `sol01.mp4` re-encoded in place: the back-office patient record showed a
 *   name, `@smile2impress.com` address and Spanish mobile. That panel is now
 *   blurred; the filename is unchanged so the fix reaches the live URL.
 * - `usertesting.mp4` was deleted and purged from git history on 2026-08-04
 *   (IM-38, the single approved force-push), then RESTORED unchanged on
 *   2026-08-05: the owner confirmed the participants consented to public use
 *   (IM-40). It is byte-identical to the original and now sits in
 *   Explorations. It shows a Zoom call with named participants and faces,
 *   the owner's former personal address, and a patient mid-procedure — all
 *   published on the owner's consent assertion, which is the record. Do not
 *   remove it on privacy grounds without asking him; do not re-purge history.
 *   Pre-purge bundle: ~/Archives/juryalyavdin-pre-history-purge-2026-08.bundle.
 * - `sol02` alt corrected: it is the kit onboarding ("what's inside", the
 *   retractor), not an aligner fit-and-change animation.
 * - Still orphaned by substance: `results01.png` (bakes in the generalized
 *   figures), `results02.png` (screenshot of someone else's article),
 *   `customer.png`, `first-time.png` (misfiled Idealista asset).
 *
 * GA4: section titles changed and the page went from 5 blocks to 6 on
 * 2026-08-04, so `section_id` values are new from that date and
 * `section_index` shifts after Problem. Schema unchanged.
 * 2026-08-05: middle section titles reverted to generic stage headers
 * (Research / Explorations / Solution) by owner decision; those `section_id`
 * values mint anew again from this date.
 *
 * The 3 videos are the "native clip" pattern — .mp4 with no .webm/poster
 * sibling — so they pass `controls webm={false} poster={false}`.
 */
import CaseStudyLayout from '../../layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro';
import ContentBlock from '../../components/ContentBlock.astro';
import ContentMedia from '../../components/ContentMedia.astro';
import StatsList from '../../components/StatsList.astro';
---

<CaseStudyLayout
  title="Impress App — first patient app case study · Jury Alyavdin"
  description="Impress had clinics, aligners and no app. I proposed and designed its first patient app: the monitoring loop shipped in three months and took the fortnightly check-up out of the clinic."
  path="/casestudy/impress-app.html"
  h1="Designing Impress's first patient app around a photo a clinician could sign off"
  avatarImage="/casestudy/impress-app/logo.png"
  orbitTop="Impress"
  orbitBottom="Orthodontic service"
  glow="#f8d8d8"
  heroImage="/casestudy/impress-app/hero.png"
  heroFit="contain"
  details={[
    { label: 'Platform', value: 'iOS, Android' },
    { label: 'Role', value: 'Founding Product Designer → Design Lead' },
    { label: 'Date', value: '2021–2023' },
  ]}
>
  <ContentBlock title="Overview">
    <p>
      Impress runs its own chain of orthodontic clinics across Europe, straightening teeth with
      clear aligners instead of braces — more than 50,000 patients as of 2023. Everything happened
      in a chair, including a check-up every fortnight for the length of the treatment. There was
      no patient app. I proposed one, argued its economics, and designed it: I was the company's
      first product designer and the only one on the project at the start; the call centre ran the
      interviews, the orthodontists held the veto over anything clinical.{' '}
      <strong class="lead-in">The result:</strong> the first version shipped in three months, took
      the fortnightly check-up out of the clinic, and is still live largely as designed.
    </p>
    <StatsList
      items={[
        { label: 'Origin', value: 'Proposed, not assigned' },
        { label: 'First version', value: 'Shipped in three months' },
        { label: 'Then', value: 'A release every fortnight' },
        { label: 'Adoption', value: 'Most patients within six months' },
      ]}
    />
    <div style={`height: var(--space-block)`} />
    <p>Impress is a physical service before it is a digital one — its own clinics, its own chairs. That is why an app was a business case to argue rather than a feature to request:</p>
    <ContentMedia
      src="/casestudy/impress-app/impress-clinic.jpeg"
      alt="Curved plywood partitions inside an Impress clinic, a treatment chair visible through an open door"
      width={1400}
      height={800}
    />
  </ContentBlock>

  <ContentBlock title="Problem">
    <p>
      Treatment runs in fortnightly steps — Impress said patients could typically see results in as
      little as six months — and each step needs a professional to confirm the teeth have actually
      moved before the patient switches to the next aligner. In practice that confirmation meant a
      clinic visit. Once acquisition and clinic overheads were counted, a single appointment cost
      the company tens to hundreds of euros, and clinic capacity was being spent on visits that
      lasted minutes. Patients paid in time. As one put it, roughly: I can't take a
      day off for five minutes in a chair.
    </p>
    <p>
      Nobody asked me to fix this. I put the case together myself — what the recurring visit cost,
      what removing it would be worth, and what would have to be true for a clinician to accept it
      — and proposed an app before there was one to design.
    </p>
    <p>This is the appointment in question — a clinician, a scanner, and a chair, repeating every fortnight for the length of the treatment:</p>
    <ContentMedia
      src="/casestudy/impress-app/patient.jpeg"
      alt="A gowned clinician using an intraoral scanner on a patient seated in a clinic chair"
      width={2000}
      height={1333}
    />
    <div style={`height: var(--space-block-lg)`} />
    <p>Consultation, plan, manufacturing and fitting happen once; the check-up in the middle repeats for the length of the treatment:</p>
    <a href="/casestudy/impress-app/journey01.png" aria-label="Open the full-size treatment lifecycle diagram">
      <ContentMedia
        src="/casestudy/impress-app/journey01.png"
        alt="Six-stage diagram: consultation, custom plan, manufacturing, onboarding, check-ups, duration"
        width={2600}
        height={1608}
      />
      <span class="media-note">Open full size</span>
    </a>
  </ContentBlock>

  <ContentBlock title="Research">
    <p>
      I wrote a structured interview script and the call centre ran it live with existing patients,
      on the phone, in Spanish. Five things came back consistently: the fortnightly check-ups
      disrupted people's schedules, waiting rooms ate time even with an appointment, patients wanted
      to watch their own progress, they were unsure when they were allowed to switch aligners, and
      booking was harder than it needed to be.
    </p>
    <p>Mapping the treatment stage by stage gave those complaints a shape — pain at every step from the first visit to the final fitting, with the recurring clinic visit running through all of it:</p>
    <a href="/casestudy/impress-app/journey02.png" aria-label="Open the full-size journey map">
      <ContentMedia
        src="/casestudy/impress-app/journey02.png"
        alt="Journey map with patient quotes and pain points across every stage of treatment"
        width={2880}
        height={1188}
      />
      <span class="media-note">Open full size</span>
    </a>
    <p>
      Four of those five described friction around the visit; only one was the visit. Booking,
      waiting rooms, messaging and progress anxiety would each have made the same treatment easier
      to sit through, and none of them would have removed a single appointment. I had three months,
      and I had argued the case on what that appointment cost — so the first release shipped one
      loop and nothing else: capture a photo good enough for a clinician to judge, get it reviewed,
      and log the wear time that made the judgement safe.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong class="lead-in">What lost: shipping the whole patient journey at once.</strong> Kit
      tracking, onboarding, a messenger and a new booking flow, all in one release — the
      complete-product launch, and the plausible call, because most of that list came from a
      patient. It would have put months between the argument I had made and the first appointment it
      removed, and it would have spent them without knowing whether a clinician would accept a
      photograph at all.
    </p>
    <p>The deferred half shipped later, driven from the console the clinicians worked in: the treatment plan is authored there — how many steps, how many days each, which ones still need an in-clinic procedure — and the patient's app follows it.</p>
    <ContentMedia
      kind="video"
      controls
      webm={false}
      poster={false}
      src="/casestudy/impress-app/sol01"
      alt="Clinician console authoring a treatment plan beside the patient app showing kit delivery and booking"
      width={1280}
      height={684}
    />
  </ContentBlock>

  <ContentBlock title="Explorations">
    <p>
      Letting patients photograph their own teeth for a clinician to review remotely was the obvious
      shape. The orthodontists' objection was not about tooling. Their licence rests on what they can
      assess, and as one of them put it, roughly: I can't sign off on what I can't examine.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong class="lead-in">What lost: live video consultations.</strong> A scheduled call with
      the orthodontist was the other credible remote model, and the one telehealth had already
      proven elsewhere — but it puts the appointment back into both calendars, it scales exactly as
      badly as a chair does, and a video stream does not give a clinician the thing they compare: a
      still, at a fixed angle, with the cheeks held back, set against the same shot from a fortnight
      ago.
    </p>
    <p>Benchmarking the field showed how hard that shot is — the established remote-monitoring products ship a moulded plastic retractor whose only job is holding it steady:</p>
    <a href="/casestudy/impress-app/benchmarking.png" aria-label="Open the full-size competitor benchmark">
      <ContentMedia
        src="/casestudy/impress-app/benchmarking.png"
        alt="Competitor remote-monitoring products: a scanning device with a moulded retractor, a patient app and a clinician dashboard"
        width={2600}
        height={1192}
      />
      <span class="media-note">Open full size</span>
    </a>
    <p>
      That made the photograph the design problem. A clinically usable photo needs specific angles,
      a cheek retractor held in place, and enough light — taken by an untrained person, in a
      bathroom, on a phone. A picture that misses those is worthless to the clinician reviewing it,
      however good the software around it is. So the app's real job was teaching capture: a guided
      sequence, one shot at a time, that a patient could get right on their own, first time.
    </p>
    <p>The capture flow walks through each shot the clinician needs, then plays it back against the last one:</p>
    <ContentMedia
      src="/casestudy/impress-app/sol04.png"
      alt="Retractor-guided photo capture screens beside a before-and-after comparison view"
      width={2320}
      height={1240}
    />
    <p>Watching patients test the prototype is what settled the guidance: a retractor in one hand and a phone in the other leaves nobody free to read instructions, so every prompt had to survive being glanced at:</p>
    <ContentMedia
      kind="video"
      controls
      webm={false}
      poster={false}
      src="/casestudy/impress-app/usertesting"
      alt="Remote testing session: a patient holds a cheek retractor and phone while the prototype guides each shot"
      width={1280}
      height={1022}
    />
    <p>Onboarding had to teach the retractor before it could teach the photo — so when it arrived in a later release it led with the kit, modelled in 3D and animated from the angle a patient actually sees:</p>
    <ContentMedia
      kind="video"
      controls
      webm={false}
      poster={false}
      src="/casestudy/impress-app/sol02"
      alt="Kit onboarding: 3D-rendered box opening, with a contents list naming aligner packs and the cheek retractor"
      width={1280}
      height={684}
    />
  </ContentBlock>

  <ContentBlock title="Solution">
    <p>
      With photos arriving reliably, a cheaper design came within reach: let the app judge the
      images and tell patients when to advance. It would have been faster for the patient and
      cheaper for the company, and it is what a pure software product would have done.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong class="lead-in">What lost: letting the app advance patients on its own.</strong> A
      wrong call about tooth movement is a clinical harm, not a poor experience, and nobody on the
      clinical side was going to take that on.
    </p>
    <p>
      So every transition waits on a clinician. The patient uploads, the orthodontist reviews, and
      only then does the next aligner become available. Patient photographs are health data —
      special-category data under GDPR Article 9 — which set the terms for moving them from a phone
      to a clinician's screen.
    </p>
    <p>
      The other half of the loop was evidence. Aligners only work if they are worn around 22 hours
      a day, the standard clinical protocol, so the app logs wear time and reminds patients —
      giving the reviewing clinician something firmer than recollection to go on.
    </p>
    <p>
      Two rounds of testing with existing patients shaped it, and the second is where the request
      for personal progress tracking came from. The daily log is what answered it, and it does
      double duty — the patient watches their own streak, the clinician gets a wear record instead
      of a recollection. Shown here as the app stood in 2023, many releases on:
    </p>
    <ContentMedia
      kind="video"
      controls
      webm={false}
      poster={false}
      src="/casestudy/impress-app/sol03"
      alt="Daily wear-time log with a timer, step counter and reminders to keep aligners in"
      width={1280}
      height={684}
    />
  </ContentBlock>

  <ContentBlock title="Outcome">
    <p>
      <strong class="lead-in">Shipped in three months, then every fortnight:</strong> a large share
      of patients were on it within months, and most were using it within six. Taking those
      appointments out of the clinics produced net savings in the low hundreds of thousands of euros
      a year once development costs were counted, and the app was well rated on both stores.
    </p>
    <StatsList
      items={[
        { label: 'The check-up', value: 'Clinic chair → phone camera' },
        { label: 'Clinic capacity', value: 'Spent on treatment, not confirmations' },
        { label: 'Cost per visit removed', value: 'Tens to hundreds of euros' },
        { label: 'Operating cost', value: 'Net savings, low hundreds of thousands of euros a year' },
      ]}
    />
    <div style={`height: var(--space-block)`} />
    <p>
      That result paid for everything I did afterwards. An app that removed a recurring cost made
      the argument for design as a function rather than a service: the back-office suite the clinics
      ran on followed from it, and so did the design team I built and led. In July 2022 Impress
      raised a{' '}
      <a href="https://techcrunch.com/2022/07/11/barcelonas-impress-heads-towards-a-125m-series-b-round-in-its-bid-to-digitize-orthodontics">$125 million Series B</a>{' '}
      — company context rather than the app's doing. Figures from Impress's internal reporting are
      given directionally here.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong class="lead-in">What it cost:</strong> the patients who most needed a chair. The
      workflow was built around the common case, and treatments that genuinely needed hands-on
      assessment were served worse by a process designed for a photograph. Catching those cases
      early, instead of waiting for a clinician to spot them at review, is where I would start if I
      picked this up again.
    </p>
  </ContentBlock>
</CaseStudyLayout>
